2026-09-03T18:51Z claude note RED-TEAM BLOCKER FIXED: census()'s walker used Path.glob("*/" + CACHE_NAME), whose internal recursion silently swallows PermissionError while scanning a subdirectory it cannot enter -- a permission-denied attachment directory vanished from every count with no error, making the committed "unreadable_caches: 0" indistinguishable between "fully scanned" and "silently skipped" (reproduced by the reviewer via chmod 0o000). Fixed by walking storage.iterdir() explicitly, which only needs permission on storage itself, then letting each child's real read failure surface through the existing per-cache try/except OSError. Reproduced the reviewer's exact case before and after: before, the denied directory disappeared silently; after, unreadable_caches correctly reads 1 with the key recorded. Added test_permission_denied_directory_is_reported_unreadable as a positive control for the walker's completeness (distinct from the existing, now-renamed test_undecodable_bytes_are_reported_not_swallowed, which only ever covered the decode-error path). All 12 tests pass (11 pass, 1 skip -- ftfy absent, same as every reviewer's environment), ruff clean. padme's real census re-run not yet repeated with the fix; the committed artifact's unreadable_caches: 0 was very likely already correct (this is the author's own library, a chmod-000 attachment dir is implausible) but now for a reason the code can attest to, not by construction of a walker that could not see failures.
--- body ---
## Context

Zoteus advertises as the all-in-one MCP server for a Zotero library. The
2026-08-30 presence-probe ruling covers the cache that *vanished*; this ticket
covers the cache that *survived but is poor* — produced by an older extractor
generation and improvable by a current one. The author estimates roughly half
of his library's full-text cache is in that state.

Two established facts frame the design space:

- **Zotero offers no re-extraction API.** X6 measured it
  (`bench/results/0025-x6-version-dynamics/`): deleting a cache forces
  unavailability, not re-extraction; nothing re-extracts headless; the local
  API documents no reindex endpoint. Healing through the platform is the
  user's Reindex, one item at a time.
- **Upstream already extracts locally when the platform cache is absent**
  (v1.10.0, upstream PR #29). So the improvement path for an all-in-one
  server needs no Zotero API at all: extend the existing local-extraction
  path from "cache absent" to "cache flagged poor", with the derivation
  recorded. The AGPL/MIT boundary already ruled out reusing Zotero's own
  chunker code (ticket 0140's log); the same applies to its extractor —
  adoptable as specification, not as source.

## What makes this a design question, not a feature request

- **Detection.** The census and content endpoints expose versions and text,
  not extractor identity. "Old extractor" must be inferred: candidates
  include the cache sidecar's recorded fields, cache mtime against
  attachment mtime, and text-quality signals (missing text layer, mangled
  ligatures/diacritics, equation dropout — the EDM experience says a text
  layer can silently drop the mathematics). The author's "roughly half" is
  a testable population claim, not yet a measurement.
- **Preference policy.** When an item's platform cache is flagged poor, does
  zoteus index its own extraction instead? Always, opt-in, or per-item? This
  is a ruling for the ledger's contract (the served text would no longer be
  what Zotero serves), and it must not silently diverge from the platform:
  the divergence is exactly what the ledger exists to record.
- **Derivation identity.** Re-extraction with a better extractor changes
  passage content without any source change — the inverse of cache-lost. The
  extractor generation therefore belongs in the derivation key (the chunker
  key already carries the resolved budget, ticket 0140), so an upgrade
  regenerates cleanly and two generations never mix unlabeled in one index.
- **Budget.** Extraction is the expensive phase (the monster document's
  extracted text is tens of MiB); re-extracting half a library is a bounded
  background campaign, not a tick — the same walk-and-budget discipline as
  SPEC.md §5.2.4's sweeps.

## Coordination with current work

- `DECISIONS.md` 2026-08-30 (presence probe) — sibling ruling; the
  cache-lost machinery (probe, stored states, surfaced counts) is the
  natural carrier for a poor-cache flag too.
- Ticket 0033 (scoped issue A) — the ledger/freshness design this would
  extend; the upstream conversation about extraction quality belongs in that
  scoped-issue lane, within GOVERNANCE.md's bounds.
- Ticket 0019 — terminal-state vocabulary the flag would reuse.
- Upstream #29 — the local-extraction path this would extend; any upstream
  ask points there.

## Actions

1. Measure the population: a probe over the real library classifying each
   cached extraction (sidecar fields, mtimes, quality heuristics), testing
   the "roughly half" estimate. Artifact to `bench/results/`, no library
   mutation.
2. From the measurement, propose the detection heuristic and its false-flag
   rate — a wrong "poor" flag re-extracts needlessly (cost), a wrong "fine"
   flag leaves bad text served (the current state, so the floor is known).
3. Draft the ruling question for the author: preference policy and its
   contract wording (DECISIONS.md, awaiting ratification).
4. If ratified: design edit (derivation key gains extractor identity; the
   background campaign's budget) and the upstream-facing text via the
   scoped-issue lane.

## Test

- The population probe runs against a fixture directory with a known mix of
  old-style and current-style caches and classifies both correctly before it
  touches the real library (positive control first).

## Verification

- [x] Population measured on the real library, artifact committed with
      provenance — `bench/results/0480-fulltext-quality/census.json`, padme
      2026-09-03; 8 211 of 8 552 PDF caches pre-form-feed (96,0 %), not the
      estimated half
- [x] Detection heuristic proposed with measured false-flag behaviour — the
      in-band form feed, false-flag ceiling 3,4 % (282 short-but-real caches);
      the mtime arm of ticket 0120's candidate is dead on this library and is
      dropped. Report: `verification/FULLTEXT-QUALITY-0480.md`
- [x] Ruling question filed in DECISIONS.md awaiting ratification
- [ ] The author has ruled (exit criteria) — pending

## Invariants

- No mutation of the author's Zotero storage or library by any probe.
- `make check` stays green; measurements quoted in prose are declared in the
  figure guard.

## Exit criteria

- The author has ruled on the preference policy, and the ruling's
  consequences (design edit, upstream item or explicit not-filed) are
  recorded — or the measurement shows the population is too small to justify
  the machinery, recorded as the finding.
